> even trying to learn it.

Hear, hear. Lojban seems to have had way, way more feedback from actual
learners and attempting speakers of the language than Loglan because of
the fact that the Loglan learning materials are not in the public domain,
but the Lojban materials are. This seems to have greatly enriched Lojban as a
language compared to Loglan, and it also means that more hands have made
lighter work in the Lojban project, enabling Lojban to progress with leaps
and bounds where Loglan development is limited to only a very few people
and progress seems slower.

